.docname {Communication & Data Flow}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Tick | Everything one building reports at one moment | One batch of readings |
    | Pub/sub | Send to whoever listens now; nobody listening, message gone | Redis channel |
    | Topic | A log that keeps messages until read | Kafka `alerts` |
    | Consumer group | Readers sharing one topic's work | `alerts` |
    | Claims header | Who the caller is, set by the edge | `x-gateway-claims` |
    | HMAC | A signature proving a shared key was used | `X-Signature` |
    | Dead-letter topic | Where messages that cannot be handled wait | `alerts.dlq` |

How the services talk, and why each talk looks the way it does.

---

## Pick by what happens if the message is lost

| Style | Used for | If lost |
|---|---|---|
| HTTP with claims | A person is waiting | The person sees an error |
| HTTP with HMAC | No person involved, like provisioning | Retried on the next round |
| Redis pub/sub | Live readings, in-app alerts | Nothing: the next tick replaces it |
| Kafka | Breaches, building registration, Wi-Fi ticks | Not lost, read after a restart |

**Example.** `dashboard` restarts for 10 seconds. Readings of those 10 seconds never reach its channel. Fine: a new tick comes, and a client that reconnects asks `telemetry` for the latest values. A breach in those 10 seconds waits in Kafka, because nothing else would remember it.

---

## Who is calling

| Caller | Proof | Checked by |
|---|---|---|
| A person | Cookie with a JWT | The edge, once. Services read `x-gateway-claims` |
| A service acting for a person | Same header, forwarded unchanged | The receiving service, for what it allows |
| A service, no person | `X-Signature`, shared secret | The receiver |
| A device or collector | `X-Signature`, key of the building | `telemetry` |

Full details: [Trust & Service Mesh](service-mesh.qd).

---

## Calls between services

| From | To | Call | Proof | Why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `digital-twin` | `telemetry` | `PUT /thresholds/buildings/{id}` | Claims | Keep the limit in step after an edit |
| `digital-twin` | `dashboard` | `POST /preferences/init/{id}` | Claims | Starting columns, best effort |
| `digital-twin` | `notification` | `POST /trigger` | System claims | Report a failed provisioning |
| `notification` | `digital-twin` | `GET /domain/{id}` | Claims | Who owns this building |
| `telemetry` | `digital-twin` | `GET /domain/{id}` | Claims | Who may read this building |
| `socket` | `digital-twin` | `GET /domain/{id}` | Claims | May this client subscribe |
| `dashboard` | `telemetry`, `digital-twin` | `GET /contracts` | None | Collect the metric catalog |
| `chat` | `agent` | `POST /ask` | Claims | Answer a question as that person |
| `agent` | `digital-twin`, `telemetry` | Read routes | Claims | Tool calls, under the person's own rights |
| `claims-gateway` | `tenancy` | `/internal/memberships`, `/internal/provision` | HMAC | Once per sign-in, never per request |
| `provisioner` | `registry`, `tenancy` | `/internal/...` | HMAC | Turn a signup into an organization |
| `telemetry` | Simulators | `/control/*` | None | Start and stop simulated buildings |

`registry` and `tenancy` only receive. Only `claims-gateway` talks to Keycloak.

---

## Messages

### Redis channels

| Channel | From | To | Carries |
|---|---|---|---|
| `telemetry:raw` | `telemetry` | `dashboard` | One envelope per tick |
| `telemetry:filtered:{buildingId}` | `dashboard` | `socket` | Same bytes, per building |
| `notifications` | `notification` | `socket` | One alert, for one organization |
| `twin:devices:{buildingId}` | `digital-twin` | `socket` | Estimated device dots, no ids, never stored |

### Kafka topics

| Topic | From | To | Group | Carries |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `alerts` | `telemetry` | `notification` | `alerts` | One message per breach of a field |
| `alerts.dlq` | `notification` | Nobody yet | | Alerts that could not be handled |
| `building-registration-requested` | `digital-twin` | `telemetry` | `building-registrations` | New building and its rooms |
| `building-registration-completed` | `telemetry` | `digital-twin` | `building-registrations-completed` | `ready` or `failed` |
| `wifi-observations` | `telemetry` | `digital-twin` | `wifi-observations` | Received Wi-Fi ticks, kept 10 min |
| `estimated-occupancy` | `digital-twin` | `telemetry` | `estimated-occupancy` | Room counts, stored as readings |

All names come from `schemas/`, never typed by hand in a service.

.box {Two services never share a group} type:{warning}
    A group shares the work: each partition goes to one member. These topics have one partition, so in a shared group one service would read everything and the other nothing. Copies of one service do share a group.

.box {Renaming a group replays the topic} type:{warning}
    No saved position means reading from the start. Harmless for registration, since every write is an upsert. For `alerts`, old breaches go out again as real pushes, limited only by the cooldown. Wait for retention to pass, or move the new group to `latest` first.

---

## Flows

### A reading reaches the screen

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant S as Sensor
        participant T as telemetry
        participant D as dashboard
        participant W as socket
        participant U as Browser
        S->>T: POST /ingest, one batch, signed
        Note over T: check signature, validate, store
        T-->>S: 202
        T->>D: telemetry:raw
        Note over D: read buildingId only
        D->>W: telemetry:filtered:{id}
        W->>U: "telemetry" to room building:{id}

- One bad reading refuses the whole batch. At most 500 readings.
- `202` means stored, so the data can be queried.
- `dashboard` and `socket` pass the bytes on, unread. Columns are applied in the browser, so the 3D twin still gets every metric.

### A breach reaches a person

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant T as telemetry
        participant N as notification
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        participant W as socket
        participant P as Push provider
        participant U as Person
        Note over T: a value is over its limit
        T->>N: Kafka alerts
        Note over N: cooldown set? stop here
        N->>Tw: GET /domain/{id}
        Tw-->>N: northfield-university
        N->>W: Redis notifications
        W->>U: "notification" to room domain:{name}
        N->>P: Web Push
        P->>U: phone or laptop notification

- Same message, two roads: bell in the app, push outside it.
- The push road needs no open tab.
- Delivery can happen twice, so a Redis cooldown of 300 s absorbs repeats.
- Failed lookup: parked on `alerts.dlq`, cooldown not set.
- No owner: dropped, and counted.

### A building is registered

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant A as Ada
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        participant T as telemetry
        A->>Tw: upload
        Note over Tw: validate, then save the job
        Tw-->>A: accepted, with a handle
        Note over Tw: worker builds the twin
        Tw->>T: building-registration-requested
        Note over T: store rooms and names
        T->>Tw: building-registration-completed
        Note over Tw: job is ready or failed

- Validation runs before the answer, so a bad file is refused at once.
- Each side commits only after handling, and every write is an upsert. Repeats change nothing.
- `resync` sends the request again when `telemetry` lost a building.

### Wi-Fi ticks

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        C["Collector"] --> T["telemetry: check, count"]
        T -- "wifi-observations" --> Tw["digital-twin: estimate"]
        Tw -- "twin:devices" --> S["socket"]
        Tw -- "estimated-occupancy" --> T

Bytes go on as received, and unknown fields are refused, so a stray MAC address cannot reach Kafka. Details: [Wireless People Flow](../design/wireless-flow.qd).

### Sign-in

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant U as Person
        participant G as claims-gateway
        participant K as Keycloak
        participant N as tenancy
        U->>K: sign in, OIDC with PKCE
        K-->>U: code
        U->>G: exchange
        G->>K: verify
        G->>N: memberships, HMAC
        Note over G,N: none yet? provision one
        G-->>U: cookie with the internal JWT, 15 min

Later requests need no call to `tenancy`. The app renews every 10 minutes.

### Signup

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant C as New customer
        participant R as registry
        participant P as provisioner
        participant N as tenancy
        C->>R: POST /organizations
        R-->>C: provisioning
        loop every 15 s
            P->>R: anything pending?
        end
        P->>N: create the domain
        P->>R: ready or failed

A `409` from `tenancy` counts as success, so a repeat is safe.

### A question to the assistant

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        participant U as Browser
        participant C as chat
        participant A as agent
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        participant T as telemetry
        U->>C: question
        C->>A: POST /ask, same claims
        A->>Tw: tool call, same claims
        A->>T: tool call, same claims
        A-->>C: answer
        C-->>U: stream

The same access rules apply as if the person had called directly. The assistant holds no rights of its own.
